const body = `<h1>2.7a — Character Creator: StatBlock</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> RPG Integration. You built a Dice utility class in Unit 1.
    Now you build a <code>Character</code> class on top of it. This is the largest build
    so far — a full 3-day assignment — and every later unit adds to it.
</p>

<p>
    The rules are <strong>Dungeons &amp; Dragons 5th edition</strong>, trimmed where full
    5e would need tools you have not met yet. The ability modifier, hit points, armor
    class, initiative, and the proficiency table are all the real 5e formulas.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>
        Create a <code>Character.java</code> class that represents an RPG character:
        a name, a level, six ability scores, hit points, armor class, and a formatted
        character sheet. The dice are rolled by the <code>Dice.java</code> engine you
        finished in 1.7.
    </p>

    <h3>The Setup</h3>

    <p>Three files. You write the first one:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><code>Character.java</code> — the character. This is today's build.</li>
        <li><code>Dice.java</code> — your engine from 1.7, in the same folder.</li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/CharacterCheck.java" download>CharacterCheck.java</a> —
            the acceptance test. Download it; you do not write it.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        You will also want a <code>CharacterTester.java</code> with a <code>main</code>
        method for your own experiments. That file is yours — nothing tests it.
    </p>

    <h3>How to Run the Acceptance Test</h3>

    <p>
        Download <a href="../data/CharacterCheck.java" download>CharacterCheck.java</a>,
        put it beside your <code>Character.java</code> and <code>Dice.java</code>, and
        from a terminal in that folder run:
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java Character.java CharacterCheck.java
java CharacterCheck</code></pre>
    <p>
        The first command compiles all three files together. The second runs the tests
        and prints a <code>PASS</code> or <code>FAIL</code> for every check. The last
        line counts them:
    </p>
    <pre><code>44 passed, 0 failed
Your Character class meets the spec.</code></pre>
    <p>
        You cannot pass all of these until you have built every method in 2.7a, 2.7b,
        and 2.7c — so expect <code>FAIL</code> lines at the start, and expect the test
        not to compile until every method exists. Run it again after each method you
        finish and watch the failures shrink. The
        <a href="../docs/unit-02/character-creator.html">full Character Creator guide</a>
        walks through the setup in more detail.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>If the test will not compile</strong>, read the error. It names the
        method or the field it could not find — which means your <code>Character.java</code>
        does not match this lesson yet. Fix your file, not the test file.
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: modifier(int score) — Score to Modifier</h3>

    <p>
        This is the 5e rule underneath everything else in this build. An ability score of
        14 is not a +14 to anything; it is a +2. Learn this method and every derived value
        below becomes a single line.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public int modifier(int score)</code></li>
        <li>Returns <code>(score - 10) / 2</code></li>
        <li>Works above and below 10 — a score of 8 gives −1, a score of 6 gives −2</li>
        <li>Do not round up: a score of 11 is a +0 in 5e, not a +1</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>
            Why subtract 10? Because 10 is the score of an average person, so 10 has to
            come out to +0 — no bonus, no penalty. Why divide by 2? Because 5e makes each
            +1 cost two points of score. Work out the modifier for 10, 12, and 20 before
            you write any code.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("14 -> " + c.modifier(14));  // +2
System.out.println("10 -> " + c.modifier(10));  // +0
System.out.println(" 8 -> " + c.modifier(8));   // -1</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run it. Is 18 a +4? Is 4 a −3? If 14 comes back as 14 you skipped the arithmetic;
        if 18 comes back as 8 you skipped the division.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 2: The Fields — What a Character IS</h3>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Two identity fields: <code>String name</code> and <code>int level</code></li>
        <li>
            Six ability scores, all <code>int</code>:
            <code>strength, dexterity, constitution, intelligence, wisdom, charisma</code>
        </li>
        <li>
            Six derived values, all <code>int</code>:
            <code>hp, maxHp, ac, initiative, proficiencyBonus, passivePerception</code>
        </li>
        <li>
            All of them <code>public</code> in this unit, for reasons the callout below
            explains
        </li>
    </ul>

    <pre><code>public class Character {

    public String name = "Unnamed";
    public int level = 1;

    public int strength, dexterity, constitution;
    public int intelligence, wisdom, charisma;

    public int hp, maxHp, ac, initiative;
    public int proficiencyBonus, passivePerception;
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        The <code>= "Unnamed"</code> and <code>= 1</code> are <strong>field
        initializers</strong>: the value a field holds before anything is assigned to it.
        Without them a new <code>String</code> field would be <code>null</code>, and your
        character sheet would print <code>null</code> for a name.
    </p>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>These fields are <code>public</code> on purpose, and it is a shortcut
        we will pay for later.</strong> In a finished class the fields are
        <code>private</code> and the outside world reads them through getters. You will
        do exactly that in Unit 5, to this exact file. For now public fields keep the
        ceremony out of your way while you learn what an object <em>is</em>.
        <br><br>
        One consequence you meet today: the acceptance test reads
        <code>hero.strength</code> and <code>hero.maxHp</code> directly. Your field
        <em>names</em> are part of the contract. Rename <code>hp</code> to
        <code>hitPoints</code> and the test stops compiling.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why are these <em>instance</em> variables rather than static? What would
                break if two characters shared one static <code>strength</code>?</li>
            <li>Why does HP need two variables? What is the difference between
                <code>hp</code> and <code>maxHp</code>?</li>
            <li>Six of these fields are <em>derived</em> — computed from other fields,
                never rolled or set by hand. Which six? Name each one's inputs.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: generateStats() — Summon the Dice God</h3>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public void generateStats()</code></li>
        <li>Rolls six ability scores with <code>Dice.statRoll()</code> from Unit 1 — one call gives you all six</li>
        <li>
            Stores them in the order the array arrives:
            <code>strength, dexterity, constitution, intelligence, wisdom, charisma</code>
        </li>
        <li>Each stat is a 3d6 roll, so each lands between 3 and 18</li>
        <li>Then fills in all six derived values, and sets <code>hp</code> to <code>maxHp</code></li>
        <li>Must not touch <code>name</code> or <code>level</code> — those are not rolled</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The derived values, in 5e terms:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Value</th><th>Formula</th></tr>
        <tr><td>maxHp</td><td><code>10 + modifier(constitution)</code> — a d10 hit die at level 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>hp</td><td><code>maxHp</code> — the character starts at full health</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ac</td><td><code>10 + modifier(dexterity)</code> — unarmored</td></tr>
        <tr><td>initiative</td><td><code>modifier(dexterity)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>passivePerception</td><td><code>10 + modifier(wisdom)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>proficiencyBonus</td><td><code>2 + (level - 1) / 4</code></td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.generateStats();
System.out.println("STR " + c.strength + "  CON " + c.constitution);
System.out.println("HP  " + c.maxHp + "  AC " + c.ac);</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run it several times. Every stat should move around between 3 and 18. If all six
        are always identical, you filled them from one roll instead of six. Check your
        <code>maxHp</code> by hand: a constitution of 14 should give
        <code>10 + 2 = 12</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: The Number You Cannot Change</h3>
        <p>
            Your sheet will report <code>Proficiency: +2</code>. That is correct for a
            level 1 character, and boring — a level 9 character should show
            <code>+4</code>. So level one up:
        </p>
        <pre><code>c.level = 9;
c.printCharacterSheet();</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong>Read the sheet carefully.</strong> It says <code>Level 9</code> and
            <code>Proficiency: +2</code>. The sheet is now <em>lying</em>, and nothing
            stopped you. You wrote one field and silently broke another.
        </p>
        <p>Now fix it the only way you currently can:</p>
        <pre><code>c.level = 9;
c.generateStats();
c.printCharacterSheet();   // now +4 - but your stats were rerolled</code></pre>
        <p>
            It works, and it cost you every ability score. A level 9 character is not a
            newly born one; it earned those stats. You have just found two problems at
            once — write down both:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>You can set <code>level</code> freely. What keeps
                <code>proficiencyBonus</code> in agreement with it?</li>
            <li>The only method that recomputes the bonus also recomputes
                <em>everything else</em>. How would you change one value and repair only
                its consequences?</li>
            <li>One more oddity: <code>proficiencyBonus</code> is set inside
                <code>generateStats()</code>, a method whose job is rolling ability
                scores. What does how proficient you are have to do with what the dice
                said? That mismatch is a smell, and it is the reason Unit 5 exists — you
                will fix all of this on this file, in Unit 5.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Roll a Better Warrior</h3>
        <p>
            Instead of 3d6 for every stat, give warriors a boost: roll
            <strong>4d6</strong> for strength and constitution, but only
            <strong>2d6</strong> for intelligence. Dice's methods can do this — but
            <code>Dice.statRoll()</code> rolls all six for you, so you would have to
            build the array yourself. Careful: this breaks the acceptance test's
            3-to-18 range check on purpose. Try it in
            <code>CharacterTester.java</code>, not in <code>Character.java</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Before you run it: with 4d6 for strength, what is the <em>lowest</em> a
            warrior's strength can still be? What is the highest? Predict both, then
            check. Did your intuition match the arithmetic?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Turn a 5e ability score into a modifier with <code>(score - 10) / 2</code></li>
            <li>Explain the difference between a stored field and a derived value</li>
            <li>Write a field initializer, and say what a String field holds without one</li>
            <li>Call your own <code>modifier()</code> from another method instead of
                repeating the arithmetic</li>
            <li>Explain why setting <code>level</code> alone leaves the sheet wrong</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

